he whole distribution of wealth levels, and only depends on the total wealth in the economy. This of course is a necessary condition for the existence of a representative household."]}, "id": "BB_2", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 16.5, "y": 57.7, "width": 67.4, "height": 29.8, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 16.5, "y": 57.7, "width": 67.4, "height": 29.8, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 16.5, "y": 57.7, "width": 67.4, "height": 29.8, "rotation": 0, "text": ["On the other hand, this is a very strong restriction, because it needs to hold for all potential wealth levels w \u2208 R_+^L. In a GE model as the ones seen so far, not any wealth level is potentially feasible, since w_i = p\u03c9_i, where \u03c9_i are the (fixed) endowments of the economy, and p are the prices. Therefore, this is a strong property on aggregation."]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 35.526315789473685, "y": 0.6578947368421052, "width": 13.507401315789473, "height": 3.6184210526315788, "rotation": 0}, "id": "G_w6GacJTx", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 35.526315789473685, "y": 0.6578947368421052, "width": 13.507401315789473, "height": 3.6184210526315788, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "G_w